  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 AM EDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nA large high pressure ridge will dominate the pattern across the \nSoutheast this week. The main impact of the ridge will be abnormally \nwarm temperatures continuing each day, with highs in the upper 80s \nto mid 90s in most of the TN Valley. CHA's streak of days reaching \n90+ degrees (now at 42 days) will likely continue through the \nweekend. The \"good\" news is that with dewpoints mainly in the 65 to \n70 range each day, the Heat Index will stay under Advisory criteria, \nbut will still reach around 100 in the southern Valley. \n\nSome isolated showers are possible in the mountains and SW NC today \nthrough Thursday. Rain chances may increase slightly later in the \nweek as the ridge appears to weaken and shift west a bit, but \nshowers will still be limited mainly in the higher terrain areas. \n\nRain chances are highly uncertain next week as some models are \nshowing a tropical system moving into northern FL while a cold front \napproaches the OH Valley from the north. Confidence is low that \nthese features will have run-to-run continuity this far out.",
